   "summary": "This concluding portion of the Embassy's monthly report for January 1941 details foreign relations and domestic affairs. Diplomatic sections cover protests over bombings and trade restrictions in China, the shooting of Shanghai Municipal Council Chairman W. J. Keswick, British suspicions of German raiders using Japanese ports, successful Japanese mediation of the French Indochina-Thailand conflict, ongoing economic demands on the Netherlands East Indies, and the extension of the fisheries protocol with the Soviet Union. On internal affairs, the report outlines Prime Minister Konoe's unity consultations, the government's target of a 100 million population by 1960, new state secrecy ordinances, and proceedings of the Imperial Diet, which avoided interpellations to streamline wartime governance. It finishes with an overview of state finance, the fiscal year 1941-1942 budget, and a notable contraction in economic activity accompanied by mandatory production cuts in raw silk and expanded controls under the National General Mobilization Law.",
   "title": "Japan: Monthly Political Report for January 1941 (Part II)",
   "to": "Department of State"
  }
 ],
